01:07:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right to bring that up—and I remember the incident you're likely referring to. In September 2018, a series of explosions and fires struck dozens of homes in the Merrimack Valley of Massachusetts (Lawrence, Andover, North Andover) almost simultaneously, and it was directly tied to the gas system. Other similar, smaller-scale overpressurization events have occurred elsewhere. But here’s the key distinction: those events were **not** a chain reaction of flame traveling from house to house along a pipe. They were caused by an **overpressurization of the entire local gas distribution network** all at once. That’s a fundamentally different failure mode. Let me break down what actually happened in those cases and why it doesn’t contradict the earlier explanation: --- ### The Merrimack Valley incident: simultaneous overpressurization, not a chain of ignitions In that disaster, during a routine pipe replacement, workers accidentally left a pressure sensor line disconnected. This caused the system’s regulators to “think” the downstream pressure was low, so they fully opened to compensate. As a result, high-pressure gas from the main transmission line was not stepped down; it flooded the low-pressure distribution system serving thousands of homes. Gas inside the pipes went from the normal ~0.5 psi (residential) to as much as 75–100 psi. This massive overpressure: - Blew out gas meters and appliance regulators inside homes. - Forced gas into living spaces through appliances (stoves, water heaters, furnaces) that were never designed to handle such pressure. - Created gas/air mixtures inside multiple houses almost simultaneously. Then, any pilot light, electrical switch, or spark could ignite that mixture. That’s why several houses appeared to catch fire or explode “at the same time.” It wasn’t flame jumping from one house to the next through the gas line; it was the **same pressure surge** hitting all homes at roughly the same moment