01:08:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent follow-up—because you've spotted a seeming contradiction in the logic. If the flame can't travel back to the source, why make turning off the gas such a high priority? The answer is that firefighters are not trying to stop a backward-traveling flame; they’re cutting off the **forward flow of fuel** feeding the fire they’re already fighting, and preventing a whole new set of immediate, localized dangers. Let me break down the real reasons. --- ### 1. Stop adding high-pressure fuel to the fire they're already fighting A structure fire will often melt or break the interior gas piping (the flexible connectors to stoves, dryers, water heaters, etc.). Once that happens, a **new, high-volume leak** opens up right inside the burning building. The gas meter and service line can deliver a huge amount of fuel—far more than the original fire had. Now, instead of just burning the ordinary contents (furniture, structure), the fire has a concentrated jet of natural gas being injected directly into it. That can: - Dramatically intensify the fire. - Turn a controllable room-and-contents fire into a raging blowtorch that overwhelms the building's structural integrity much faster. - Make it impossible for firefighters to enter or approach. Shutting the gas main at the meter instantly cuts off that **new** fuel supply, removing that additional energy. --- ### 2. Prevent unburned gas from accumulating outside the fire zone A broken pipe inside a building might not be in the immediate fire area. It could be in a different part of the basement, or the leak could push gas into wall cavities, attics, or adjacent rooms that haven’t yet ignited. If that happens, unburned gas can build up to an explosive mixture. Then, if the fire spreads into that area, or a firefighter opens a door, or a spark from a collapsing wall occurs, the result is a backdraft or a secondary explosion that can kill firefighters. Turning off the gas stops that accumulation