g fire’s heat, and ignites. That’s a secondary release and ignition, not a flame traveling inside the pipe from the stove. - **Pressure surge from a ruptured pipe elsewhere:** If the stove’s gas line fails and dumps a lot of gas, it can cause a slight pressure drop in the system. But that’s not going to cause a reverse flow or ignite anything downstream. It might just starve other pilots, which would go out. No chain reaction. - **Common cause: heat from the same fire affecting all lines simultaneously.** The fire can radiate heat to multiple areas, causing simultaneous or sequential failures of plastic shutoff valves, flexible hoses, or melting of the pipe itself. So it might look like the fire “jumped” from appliance to appliance along the gas line, but it’s actually the fire’s heat traveling through the house, not flame in the pipe. ### 5. A note on “flashback” within an appliance (which is different) You might have heard of a “flashback” where a flame momentarily darts back into the burner tube and makes a “poof” sound. That can happen only *within* the mixing chamber of an appliance, where gas and air are already premixed. It’s a brief, localized event that stops at the orifice because the gas velocity there is too high. It doesn’t escape into the house piping. It’s contained entirely within that appliance’s burner assembly. --- **So, in a single house, the gas line is not a fuse leading from a flaming stove to the water heater.** The flame can’t enter or travel inside the pipe because there’s no oxygen, and the flow is relentlessly outward. Any appearance of sequential appliance ignition is really just the house fire spreading physically and melting different sections of the gas pipe one by one, each creating its own local fuel leak that ignites. The original stove fire remains the only source of heat; the gas line just becomes a series of potential high-pressure blowtorches if breached by external damage. And that’s exactly why firefighters still prioritize shutting off the gas meter—not out of fear of a pipe-based flame race, but to stop the bleeding of high-pressure fuel into every rupture the fire is causing.
