ng from building to building. These are not high-speed processes: - Gas migration underground is limited by soil permeability, pressure gradients, and the rate at which it can displace air in confined spaces. It seeps, it doesn't race. In a typical residential leak, gas might travel tens of feet through the ground in minutes, not miles. To reach an explosive concentration in a distant basement takes time—often longer than the emergency response lag. - The radiant-heat ignition of adjacent gas meters requires the fire to be so large and sustained that it physically ignites the neighbor's house. In dense urban areas, that can happen, but it's a house-to-house fire spread, which the fire department actively fights with water. The chain of building ignitions is being battled in real time, even while the gas is still on. Firefighters don't wait for the gas valves to close; they're pouring water on the exposures. That suppression slows or stops the domino effect independently of the gas flow. So even if the gas isn't shut off for an hour, the physical speed of the cascade is slow enough that the chain doesn't get very far before either human intervention (firefighting, eventual valve closure) or natural dissipation stops it. --- ### 3. The system has inherent breaks that don't depend on human speed Beyond human-operated valves, there are passive and automatic safeguards that function without anyone making a phone call: - **Pressure regulators** at the entrance to each building and at district stations are one-way devices. They reduce pressure, but they also act as a barrier. If the downstream (house) side is completely breached and burning, the regulator will flow gas at its maximum capacity, but once the downstream pressure drops enough, some regulators can fail closed or limit flow—acting as a de facto shutoff without human intervention. - **Excess flow valves (EFVs)** , where present, close mechanically and instantly when flow exceeds a threshold. No human required, no