sphere are excellent diffusers. --- ### 5. The record shows that even the worst disasters don't propagate to the source Look at the most extreme gas-related fires and explosions: San Bruno, Merrimack Valley, Buncefield (oil), etc. In none of those did the chain reaction travel more than a few city blocks. Even with response times of many minutes to over an hour, the disaster remained geographically contained. The source—the high-pressure transmission system or the production facility—was never threatened by a backward cascade. The damage was catastrophic but local. If a true source-to-house fuse existed, we would have seen an entire city blow. We haven't. --- **So to your point:** You're right that lag in valve closure means the chain reaction can get further than an idealized "instant shutoff" scenario might suggest. It can engulf several buildings, a whole block, maybe more. That's a real and terrifying local cascade. But the inherent slowness of gas migration, the passive barriers in the system, and the physical dilution over
