istance pipelines—was never threatened. The destruction, while apocalyptic within the urban area, remained geographically contained by the simple fact that the distribution system has finite extent and the transmission system is a separate, distant entity. --- ### 4. What about a cascading series of manual valve closures? In a major disaster, the gas utility's emergency plan (even a pre-digital one) involves sending crews to manually close valves at strategic points: district regulator stations, transmission gate valves, and the city border stations. The delay may be hours, and the local destruction may be vast, but the closure will eventually happen. The question is whether, in that window, the chain could get so far that it reaches a major transmission artery before the valve is turned. For that to happen, the gas migration plume would have to travel miles underground through varying soil types, cross rivers, roads, and other open spaces, all while maintaining an explosive concentration, and then find an ignition source at the transmission pipeline's location—which is typically in a rural or unpopulated area with few ignition sources. It's not physically plausible. The plume dilutes and dissipates long before then. --- ### 5. The honest, no-comfort answer In a city with an old, non-retrofitted gas network, a catastrophic earthquake would be a nightmare. The chain reactions you've described—migration, sequential ignitions, block-level conflagration—would play out with devastating effectiveness, potentially destroying large swaths of the city. The lack of automatic valves means the fuel keeps flowing longer, and the damage radius expands. But "all the way back to the source" still doesn't happen. The chain is long, but it's not infinite. It's bounded by: - The physical extent of the distribution network (which ends at the city's edge or at district regulators). - The inability of flame or meaningful gas concentrations to travel miles through soil to a separate, buried, high-pressure transmission line. - The eventual manual intervention (even if delayed) that shuts off the transmission supply. - The physics that prevent any internal pipe flame from reversing course. So, your scenario runs wild longer and wider in an older city, but it still runs into the same fundamental walls. The source—the high-pressure backbone and the gas fields—remains insulated by distance, system segregation, and the stubborn refusal of fire to travel where there's no oxygen and no continuous air-filled path. --- **The bottom line:** You've correctly identified a terrifying vulnerability in many real-world systems. In those places, a major event would produce a much larger local cascade, perhaps urban-scale, but it would not become a regional pipeline disaster. The chain is real, ugly, and can cover a lot of ground—but it has an ultimate ceiling, even without fancy valves, set by the basic layout of the gas network and the physics of combustion.
